WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.720
In the many years since Home Alone's release&nbsp;
in 1990, it's become evident that while Kevin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.360
McAllister's solo Yuletide adventures are still&nbsp;
tremendously fun to watch, there are a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.920
things about this film that are a little too&nbsp;
bizarre, illogical, or even downright disturbing.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.240
If you're a kid watching Home Alone,&nbsp;
Kevin's dialogue feels almost aspirational.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.520
The things he says to his parents and other&nbsp;
family members are the kinds of things we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.680
all wished we could say when we were young,&nbsp;
but probably never had the nerve to do so.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.480
When his mom tells him to leave&nbsp;
her room, for example, he responds:

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.280
"Hang up the phone and make me why don’t ya."

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.200
And then when she tells him to go upstairs after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.680
he shoves his brother Buzz in&nbsp;
the kitchen, he shouts back:

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.640
"I am upstairs, dummy!"

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.960
Granted, Kevin is in the midst of being punished&nbsp;
when he says this, but still, calling your mom a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.840
"dummy" and suffering zero consequences would've&nbsp;
felt like a dream for young moviegoers. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.240
as an adult, it's a little hard to overlook&nbsp;
just how rude Kevin is, and how his parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.960
either ignore it or laugh it off. To be fair, most&nbsp;
of the other McCallister kids are just as bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.520
if not worse. So Kevin doesn't really stand&nbsp;
out that much in comparison, making the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.480
of traveling to Paris with so many disrespectful&nbsp;
kids sound more like a nightmare than a vacation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.600
Still, considering that Kevin is the&nbsp;
protagonist, it's a little weird that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.680
he's characterized as being just as unpleasant to&nbsp;
be around as the rest of the kids in his family.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.840
Even if you put aside the fact that the&nbsp;
McCallisters have clearly spent a small fortune&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.000
decorating every inch of their expansive house for&nbsp;
a holiday they're not even going to be home for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.600
have you ever taken a close look at the rest&nbsp;
of that house? It's not just the seasonal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.840
decorations that make it feel festive. The&nbsp;
entire interior design scheme seems to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.480
been planned around the holiday. Just consider&nbsp;
the green kitchen counters and red pots and pans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:37.760
or the red-green-and-white patterned&nbsp;
wallpaper covering the walls of nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.880
every room and hallway. And there's&nbsp;
also the parents' red bedspread,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.640
the framed red flowers on their walls, and the&nbsp;
red and green furniture in the living room.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.880
The kitchen floor is red. The dining room&nbsp;
walls are red. The upstairs walls are green.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:52.880
The living room has a red and white rug on the&nbsp;
floor and green curtains framing the windows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.920
Even the telephone is green. From lampshades&nbsp;
to bathrobes to prominently placed teapots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.080
it's hard to find a single surface or person in&nbsp;
the McCallister house that isn't decked out for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:06.480
the holidays all the time. After living in such&nbsp;
a festive home year-round, it's no wonder the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.560
McCallister children seem to be fresh out of&nbsp;
Christmas cheer by the time December arrives.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.480
When Kevin shoves Buzz for saying&nbsp;
he ate Kevin's entire cheese pizza,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.520
Buzz falls into some cups of milk,&nbsp;
which spill all over the passports&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:21.200
and airline tickets on the kitchen counter.&nbsp;
The next morning, during the hubbub to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.800
everyone out of the house and to the airport&nbsp;
on time, Kevin's dad Peter mentions that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.160
passports are in the microwave, where&nbsp;
he put them in order to dry them off.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.880
This microwave plan was probably a bad idea&nbsp;
for several reasons. Although passports in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.800
1990 wouldn't have had the radio frequency&nbsp;
identification chips contained in modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:40.880
passports, which could've caused them to burst&nbsp;
into flames in the microwave, they still would've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:44.720
had plastic covers, which could've melted.&nbsp;
The passports also would've contained small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.560
amounts of metal in the foil stamping on their&nbsp;
covers and the inner security features, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.280
would've caused them to catch fire. Peter's lucky&nbsp;
he didn't cause the whole trip to go up in flames.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.120
Although at least in that case, Kevin wouldn't&nbsp;
have accidentally been left at home by himself.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.080
Kevin's mom Kate realizes on the plane that she&nbsp;
left Kevin behind, and the first thing she does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:06.480
after landing is wrestle a payphone away from&nbsp;
a French woman so that she can call the police.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.240
What follows is a frustrating series of&nbsp;
conversations as Kate gets transferred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:14.480
from one department to another and conveys her&nbsp;
story over and over to bored officers who seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.720
more interested in preparing for Christmas&nbsp;
than in ensuring a child's safety. If you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:22.400
a kid watching this scene, it's easy to get&nbsp;
annoyed at the police, and especially at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.880
officer who later goes to the McCallister house,&nbsp;
knocks once, then gives up and leaves when no one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.280
answers the door. Surely this police department&nbsp;
is the height of professional incompetence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:36.000
right? As it turns out, though, maybe not.&nbsp;
When Kate calls, all she says each time is:

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.280
"Yeah hi look,

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.920
I'm calling from Paris. I&nbsp;
have a son who's home alone.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:41.920
Our phones there are out of order so I'd like

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:43.920
somebody to go over to our house and tell him

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.199
that we're coming home to get him."

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.600
She never mentions Kevin's age, or the fact&nbsp;
that he's not supposed to be alone. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:53.120
she comes across as an overprotective&nbsp;
parent who purposely left her teenager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.880
at home and now wants the police to make sure&nbsp;
he isn't throwing a rager while she's away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:00.480
No wonder the police didn't bother investigating&nbsp;
further when Kevin didn't answer the door.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.880
It can seem illogical that Kevin never&nbsp;
calls the police until the end of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.800
choosing instead to construct an elaborate&nbsp;
series of booby traps to protect himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.760
But by Kevin's logic, it actually makes sense.&nbsp;
He thinks he made his family disappear by magic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.080
and when he later encounters the Wet Bandits,&nbsp;
he recognizes Harry as the police officer who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:22.000
visited his house earlier. Police can't&nbsp;
help with a magically disappearing family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.960
and they may in fact be the bad&nbsp;
guys if Harry is one of them.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:29.920
But it's less clear why the pizza guy doesn't call&nbsp;
911 after he delivers a pie to the McCallister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:34.160
home and Kevin uses the Angels with Filthy Souls&nbsp;
video to trick him into believing someone is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:38.960
trying to kill him. The ruse clearly works,&nbsp;
but then nothing happens. If he's under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.720
impression that one of his customers just tried to&nbsp;
kill him, you'd think that he'd call the police,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:46.560
or at least report it to his manager, who could&nbsp;
pass the incident along to the authorities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.400
But it appears that instead he just keeps it&nbsp;
to himself and goes about the rest of his day.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:54.480
During the break-in extravaganza at the&nbsp;
end of the film, Kevin knows to be ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.000
because the Wet Bandits inadvertently&nbsp;
tell him to expect them at nine o'clock.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.160
But when he sets up his elaborate fake party,&nbsp;
all he knows is that two creepy guys in a van&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:06.400
were hanging around outside his house earlier in&nbsp;
the day. He had no way of knowing what time they'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:10.720
be coming back, or if they were even planning&nbsp;
on returning at all. Yet he somehow managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:15.200
to be enthusiastically dancing at the precise&nbsp;
moment when Harry and Marv drove by that night.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.120
Either Kevin was incredibly lucky with his&nbsp;
timing, or he'd already been partying for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:22.800
hours with his cardboard friends by the&nbsp;
time the Wet Bandits finally returned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:26.080
The most plausible explanation is that&nbsp;
he had the whole party setup ready to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:29.760
go as soon as their van turned onto his&nbsp;
street, kept a close watch, and it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.600
a lot less complicated than it appeared&nbsp;
for him to put all the cutouts in motion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.840
But it's more fun to imagine that he was just&nbsp;
rocking out with his inanimate guests all day.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:42.000
Although the church scene between Kevin&nbsp;
and Old Man Marley is heartwarming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:46.240
it's hard not to notice that it's also a&nbsp;
little odd while watching it as an adult.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:50.160
Even if you put aside the fact that Kevin is in&nbsp;
a church an hour before the Wet Bandits are due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:54.320
to arrive and he has to run home in order to&nbsp;
make his battle plan work in time, there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:58.640
still a few elements about this scene that are&nbsp;
kind of strange when you examine them closely.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:02.560
It makes sense that Old Man Marley would come over&nbsp;
to introduce himself to Kevin and tell him that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:06.080
he doesn't need to be afraid of him. After all,&nbsp;
it would've been hard for him to miss his young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:10.720
neighbor fleeing from him in terror earlier in the&nbsp;
movie. However, it's a little bizarre that Marley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.120
then proceeds to unload all of his troubles with&nbsp;
his estranged son onto a second-grader he's only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.080
just met and whose parents are nowhere in sight.&nbsp;
Of course, we understand that Marley means well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:23.920
especially when he comes to Kevin's rescue&nbsp;
at the end of the film, but typically adults&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:27.280
don't have impromptu heart-to-hearts with&nbsp;
unaccompanied minors they don't know.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.920
Of the many traps Kevin sets for the Wet&nbsp;
Bandits, one that could've proved disastrous is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:36.400
the electric charcoal starter that he hangs from&nbsp;
the front doorknob. The idea behind it is simple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:40.480
even if the physics are a little advanced for&nbsp;
a second-grader. Kevin plugs in the starter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:44.400
inside the house, hangs it on the indoor metal&nbsp;
knob, which conducts the heat to the outdoor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:48.640
knob and ultimately sears an impression of&nbsp;
itself onto an unsuspecting Harry's hand.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:52.720
After Harry gives up on the front entrance, Kevin&nbsp;
leaves the starter plugged in and hanging on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:57.600
door. Keeping in mind that it had already been on&nbsp;
the door for about an hour before Harry showed up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:02.720
that starter was probably left unattended for at&nbsp;
least a couple hours before Kevin turned it off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:05.280
In that time, there would've&nbsp;
been a serious risk of the door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:08.240
and subsequently the entire&nbsp;
house, catching on fire.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:10.000
"It's pretty cool that you&nbsp;
didn't burn the place down."

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:14.560
The traps that Kevin sets for the Wet Bandits at&nbsp;
the end of the movie all seem like delightfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:18.400
funny pranks when you're a kid. But as an&nbsp;
adult, it's easy to see the many ways they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:22.320
could've resulted in death. Both criminals&nbsp;
take some serious blows to their heads and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:26.640
the rest of their bodies during the home invasion&nbsp;
sequence, any one of which could've proved lethal.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:31.280
First, there's the iron dropped on Marv's face&nbsp;
via the laundry shoot, which falls three stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:36.080
to hit him squarely in the forehead. That's enough&nbsp;
for severe facial trauma and bone fractures. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:40.400
there are the paint cans both Bandits take to the&nbsp;
face, which could've taken their heads right off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:44.560
And let's not forget the crowbar that Marv uses&nbsp;
to whack Harry in the chest, which could've broken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:48.560
his ribs, punctured his lungs, or pierced&nbsp;
his heart. And that's not even taking into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:53.760
account all the other injuries both men likely&nbsp;
suffered as a result of Kevin's less lethal traps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:57.920
Somehow, we doubt that the McCallisters'&nbsp;
VCRs and jewelry were worth all of that.

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:00.480
"You guys give up, or you thirsty for more?"

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:04.000
While it's understandable why the police&nbsp;
don't seem to take Kevin's plight too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:08.320
seriously earlier in the movie, it's a little&nbsp;
less easy to follow their process at the end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:12.000
After receiving Kevin's call that the Murphys'&nbsp;
house is being robbed, the police arrive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:16.560
to discover Harry and Marv significantly worse&nbsp;
for wear, presumably still being watched over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:20.800
by Old Man Marley and his shovel. Harry and&nbsp;
Marv are arrested, and Kevin watches from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:24.960
the window of his house, where he then goes to&nbsp;
bed and peacefully awaits Christmas morning.

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:29.440
Why don't the police ever question Kevin? They&nbsp;
must've wondered why the Wet Bandits were so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:33.600
beaten up, and in such a weird way, what with&nbsp;
the feathers and burn wounds on Harry and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:38.400
iron-shaped impression on Marv's face. Even if&nbsp;
Marley attempted to take responsibility for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:41.680
the Bandits were fully conscious when the&nbsp;
police showed up, and they would likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:45.200
have been grumbling about the kid that did&nbsp;
this to them. It's bizarre that no one ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:49.600
comes to get Kevin's side of the story, and in&nbsp;
the process realize that he's home by himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:53.680
If the police were better at their jobs, Kevin's&nbsp;
mom probably would've arrived home to an empty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:57.840
house and the message that her son was at the&nbsp;
police station. Sure, it would've taken away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:01.920
from the moving family reunion at the end, but&nbsp;
it would've helped with everyone's peace of mind.

